WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Bounds safety means code accesses only the elements and bytes that belong to the object being used.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.520
This discipline applies equally to stack and heap data.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.180
A local array is not safe simply because it lives on the stack.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.600
A heap buffer is not unsafe simply because it was dynamically allocated.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.420
Safety depends on whether the code preserves valid size information, checks indexes, and respects the object's lifetime.

6
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.140
Prefer types that keep bounds close to data.

7
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.520
std::array expresses fixed-size storage.

8
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.820
std::vector and std::string track dynamic size.

9
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.640
std::span can represent a bounded non-owning view when the source data outlives the span.

10
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.460
Range-based loops and standard algorithms can reduce manual index handling.

11
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.000
These patterns make review easier because the valid range is visible in the type or operation.

12
00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:01.320
Raw arrays, unchecked indexes, pointer arithmetic, and mismatched pointer-and-length pairs deserve careful attention.

13
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:10.380
Before copying, transforming, parsing, or allocating based on a size, validate that the size is sensible for the source and destination.

14
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:20.000
Be cautious with externally influenced sizes, signed and unsigned conversions, offsets, counts, and multiplication used for allocation.

15
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:28.100
Avoid assuming null termination when an API provides explicit length, and avoid inventing a length when the data format does not support one.

16
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.020
Bounds safety is a design property.

17
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.320
Good interfaces preserve length with data and make invalid ranges hard to express.

18
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:43.940
Good tests exercise empty input, maximum sizes, boundary indexes, and error paths.

19
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.200
Good reviews ask whether each access is within both lifetime and bounds.

20
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.120
When stack and heap data interact, follow the size relationship explicitly.

21
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.700
A stack temporary may receive data copied from a heap container.

22
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.520
A heap allocation may be sized from a parsed stack-local header.

23
00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:06.460
A span may describe either storage kind.

24
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:15.200
In each case, the safe question is the same: where did the length come from, has it been validated, and does it still describe the object being accessed?
